# Answer 13 · The Status of Gender Studies in Pakistan and Views on Autonomy versus Integration (CSS 2023)
@py question 2023-2

## The answer map
::: fig mindmap | The answer map: every branch is one numbered heading of the model answer below.
@note A near repeat of 2016 Q2 — so organise the status as strengths, weaknesses, opportunities and threats, then argue a concrete dual strategy.
@ask ① WRITE A NOTE on the status of Gender Studies in Pakistan
@ask ② GIVE YOUR VIEWS on the autonomy vs integration debate
(1) Introduction: arrived as Women's Studies, grew into Gender Studies; recognised but fragile
(2) How the field grew: NGOs first; five centres 1989; renamed Gender Studies; CSS 2016
(3) Strengths: degrees to PhD; research that shaped laws; trained staff for commissions
(4) Weaknesses: donor money; few experts; borrowed theory; thin data; isolated
(5) Opportunities: CEDAW and SDG 5; demand for gender experts; 2026 gender gap data
(6) Threats: backlash; budget cuts; "gender" reduced to a checklist
(7) The debate: separate department or gender in every subject
(8) My view: keep departments; add gender course by course to other subjects
(9) Conclusion: from the margins to the centre of national life
:::

## Decoding the question
This question is almost the same as **2016 Q2**, but it says "**Gender Studies**", not "Women's Studies". Show that you know the field has moved from one to the other. **"Write a note on the status"** asks for an organised assessment; a **SWOT** structure (strengths, weaknesses, opportunities, threats) gives it quickly. **"Give your views"** asks for a clear position on the debate — best made concrete by saying **how** integration should work in Pakistan.

## The model answer

### 1. Introduction

#### i) Definition
**Gender Studies** studies how society makes gender and how gender shares out power, money and chances between women, men and gender-diverse people.

#### ii) The Pakistani journey
The field arrived in Pakistan as **Women's Studies** and has grown into the study of **gender relations**, including men and gender-diverse people.

#### iii) Why the wording matters
The examiner asked almost the same question in 2016. The change in wording — **Gender** rather than **Women's** Studies — is itself part of the answer: it shows how the field has grown since then.

#### iv) The argument
After more than three decades the field is **recognised and productive but fragile**. Its future depends on how it settles the debate between autonomy and integration. My view: Pakistan needs **both, deliberately combined**, with the departments leading the effort to carry gender into every profession.

### 2. How the field grew

#### i) Research outside the universities
Research on women began with **Shirkat Gah** (==1975==), the **Women's Action Forum** (==1981==) and the **Aurat Foundation** (==1986==).

#### ii) The centres of 1989
The **Ministry of Women's Development** created **Women's Studies centres** at **Quaid-i-Azam University** and the Universities of **Karachi, Punjab, Peshawar** and **Balochistan**.

#### iii) Growth and renaming
Most centres became **Gender Studies departments**; programmes spread to other universities; and from ==CE-2016== Gender Studies became a **CSS optional subject**. The research agenda has widened too — from women's legal status to **masculinities**, **climate and disaster**, **digital harassment** and **transgender rights**.

### 3. Strengths

- **Degrees:** BS, MPhil and PhD programmes at many public universities.
- **Research:** work on violence, honour crimes, family law, politics, work and health, in journals such as the ***Pakistan Journal of Gender Studies***.
- **Trained people:** graduates staff the **National Commission on the Status of Women** (==2000==), provincial commissions, protection centres and NGOs.
- **Impact on law:** evidence behind the laws on **harassment** (==2010==), **acid attacks** (==2011==), **honour killing** (==2016==) and **violence at home** (Punjab ==2016==; Islamabad ==2025==).

### 4. Weaknesses

- **Donor money** that comes and goes with projects.
- **Few experts**, especially PhD-qualified teachers outside the big cities.
- **Borrowed theory:** mostly Western books in English; little theory from Pakistan's own history and religious traditions.
- **Thin data:** small studies; little long-term research.
- **Isolation:** mostly women students and staff; little influence on other departments.

### 5. Opportunities

- **Promises to keep:** Pakistan's duties under **CEDAW** (joined ==1996==) and **SDG 5** need gender data and experts.
- **Rising demand:** gender units in ministries, **harassment committees** under the 2010 Act, protection authorities and private companies all need trained staff.
- **A clear agenda:** Pakistan ranked ==143rd of 145== in the Global Gender Gap Report 2026; only ==24.4%== of working-age women are in the labour force (LFS 2024–25); women's literacy is about ==53%== (Census 2023).
- **Digital reach:** online courses and open journals can reach students far from big universities.

### 6. Threats

- **Backlash:** the charge of a "**Western**" or "**anti-family**" agenda, sharpened by controversies such as the debate over the **Transgender Persons Act**.
- **Budget cuts** in public universities.
- **Co-option:** "gender" reduced to a **donor checklist** instead of an analysis of power.

::: fig grid | The status of Gender Studies in Pakistan: strengths, weaknesses, opportunities, threats.
@cols 2
Strengths: degrees to PhD; research that shaped laws; trained staff for commissions
Weaknesses: donor money; few experts; borrowed theory; isolated departments
Opportunities: CEDAW and SDG 5; demand for gender experts; clear data on gaps
Threats: backlash; budget cuts; "gender" as a checklist
:::

The picture is of a field that has **arrived but not settled**. It has proved its value; it has not yet secured its future. Its strengths and opportunities are real, but its weaknesses — **money, people and theory** — leave it exposed to backlash and budget cuts.

#### Why this matters for the debate
The weaknesses point directly to the second part of the question. The field's **isolation** is the classic risk of autonomy — ghettoisation. Its **thin reach** into law, medicine and economics shows how little integration has happened. So the status of the field and the autonomy–integration debate are **two sides of one problem**.

### 7. The autonomy versus integration debate

#### i) Origin
**Gloria Bowles and Renate Duelli Klein** (*Theories of Women's Studies*, ==1983==) set out the debate.

#### ii) Autonomy
**Autonomy** means a **separate department** with its own teachers, degrees and research. It gives **depth and a critical voice**, but risks **ghettoisation** — an ignored "women's corner".

#### iii) Integration
**Integration** means **adding gender to every subject**. It gives **reach and real change**, but risks **dilution** — one token lecture. Bowles and Klein saw autonomy as the **base** from which integration should grow.

### 8. My view: a concrete dual strategy for Pakistan

#### i) Where Pakistan stands
Pakistan has built **autonomy** but neglected **integration**: the departments exist, while the rest of the curriculum barely mentions gender.

#### ii) What integration should look like
::: table | Gender content Pakistan should integrate, subject by subject.
@widths 3.5,10.5
Subject | Gender content
Medicine and nursing | Maternal health, reproductive rights, caring for survivors of violence
Law | Family law, inheritance, the pro-women laws of 2006–2025
Economics and business | Unpaid care work, the pay gap, gender budgeting
Education | Gender-aware textbooks; keeping girls in school
Engineering and planning | Safe transport, lighting, toilets and workplaces for women
Civil-service training | Gender analysis of policy; harassment law; CEDAW and SDG reporting
:::

#### iii) Who should lead it
The **departments** should train the teachers, share the courses and supply the research. This answers the fear of **token integration**: trained specialists, approved content and Higher Education Commission support, not individual goodwill. A small start is possible at once: a **compulsory gender module** in every law, medical and teacher-training degree, taught by trained staff.

#### iv) Why it answers the critics
Gender taught inside a Pakistani **law course on inheritance** or a **medical course on maternal deaths** is plainly about Pakistan's own problems — the best reply to the "**Western agenda**" charge. It also mirrors the state's own **twin track** since Beijing (==1995==): women's institutions **plus** gender mainstreaming across ministries.

### 9. Conclusion
Gender Studies in Pakistan has real strengths — degrees, research, trained people and influence on law — but it still depends on donors, lacks experts and sits in a corner of the university. The autonomy versus integration debate should be settled in favour of **both**: departments strong enough to produce expertise and local theory, and a deliberate programme to carry gender into **every profession**. Only then will the field move **from the margins of the university to the centre of national life**.

## Why this answer scores
- It spots the **near repeat** and answers it freshly through a **SWOT** structure, shown as a figure.
- The status is **current** (2026 data; the 2025 Islamabad law) and evidenced.
- The debate is credited to its **source** and summarised precisely, with each side's risk.
- The view is **concrete**: a subject-by-subject plan, not a vague "both".
- The conclusion **joins the two parts**.
